course: dictionary
lesson: 6520
title: Keeping young features
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Neoteny is a science word for keeping young, baby-like features after you have grown up. It can happen in animals or in people.
explain: Read this: «The frills on an axolotl's head are gills: it is a salamander that keeps its young features, gills and a tail fin, for its whole life and stays in water, which scientists call neoteny.» The clues are "keeps its young features", "for its whole life" and "stays in water". The axolotl is still a grown-up, but it keeps young features, so neoteny means keeping young features after growing up.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence: "...which scientists call keeping young features after growing up." It still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
fact: Neoteny means keeping young or baby-like features after growing up.
fact: The axolotl is a Mexican salamander that keeps its feathery gills and stays in water all its life, a famous case of neoteny.
fact: Scientists say dogs show neoteny compared with wolves, with floppy ears and playfulness.
fact: Humans have flat faces and big heads, like young apes, which is also a sign of neoteny.
fact: Neoteny is an uncountable noun, so we say "some neoteny", not "a neoteny".
exercise: Neoteny means... | Growing very large very quickly | Changing colour to hide from predators | Living only on land | *Keeping young, baby-like features after growing up
exercise: Scientists say dogs show neoteny compared with wolves. Which features are a sign of this? | Thick fur that changes colour | Living in packs in the forest | *Floppy ears and playfulness | Long, sharp hunting teeth
exercise: Which animal keeps its feathery gills all its life? | The ancient oak tree | *The axolotl, a Mexican salamander | The wolf | A grown-up sheep
test: Humans have flat faces and big heads, like young apes. Which is a sign of neoteny here? | *A flat face and a big head | Fur all over the body | A hard shell covering the body | A long tail for swimming
test: The axolotl stays in water all its life. Why is this neoteny? | It lays its eggs in the sea | *It keeps a young feature, its gills, for its whole life | It is a fish that swims fast | It hunts at night
exam: Pick the best meaning of neoteny in this sentence: «The axolotl keeps its gills all its life, a famous case of neoteny.» | Losing all its colour | *Keeping young features after growing up | Hunting in packs | Sleeping through the winter
